Ernest Shult (2005) first introduced \textit{special sets} in connection to other objects of finite geometry, such as generalised quadrangles, pseudo-ovals and locally Hermitian ovoids. It is a subset of points lying in the finite Hermitian surface $\mathrm{H}(3,q^2)$ for $q$ an odd prime power that satisfies nice geometric properties. The only known examples are equivalent under the collineation group of $\mathrm{H}(3,q^2)$ to the \textit{Hermitian Veronesean} $\mathcal{V}_0$:
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{V}_0:=\{(1,t,t^q,t^{q+1}):t\in\mathbb{F}_{q^2}\}\cup\{(0,0,0,1)\}.
\end{equation*}
This curve also arises in connection to objects of algebraic geometry; it is the $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-rational points of an $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-maximal non-planar rational curve of degree $q+1$ over the algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb{F}_{q^2}}$, meaning that it has genus $0$ and meets the Hasse-Weil bound. \\

In this talk, I will discuss recent work with John Bamberg, showing that the Hermitian Veronesean is the only special set (up to equivalence), and further establish the connection between special sets and non-planar rational curves of degree $q+1$.

Every ten years, or so, it seems that I attempt a return to my happy place, \emph{complicial sets}. In the manner of Odysseus, however, I have been repeatedly distracted from these beautiful structures in favour of the sirens of \(\infty\)-\emph{cosmology}; a theory largely shaped, for me, by my hope that it might be used to develop the \emph{category theory} of complicial sets. That said, at the time we set out on our path through the \(\infty\)-cosmos I had no idea how to achieve this goal. Indeed, I should now confess that the \(\infty\)-cosmos of complicial sets introduced in {[}2{]} is, for these purposes, a complete dead end.

Since I last spoke at length on this topic, at CIRM in Luminy 2017, a very great deal of complicial water has passed under other bridges. Most notably, thanks to the work of Maehara, Rovelli, Ozornova and Loubaton, we now know that complicial sets, Ross Street's granddaddy of all proposed \((\infty,\infty)\)-categorical structures {[}3{]}, are indeed equivalent to all other known \((\infty,\infty)\)-category models.

That said there is still a lot of work to be done to tame the complicial universe. For example, we are yet to provide \emph{explicit} equivalences with some of the most widely discussed \((\infty,\infty)\)-models and, more egregiously, their category theory is a completely unwritten book.

Join me on the penteconter of this talk to recap some of these recent developments and to ponder how to build \(\infty\)-cosmoi of complicial sets that really do capture the essence of their category theory. Along the way, we may even dip our toes into the world of \emph{comical sets}, their cubical siblings {[}1{]}.

Various contributing parts of the new work I shall report on here are joint with Yuki Maehara, Emily Riehl and Jonathan Weinberger.

